{"pagination":{"maxPageableSet":"10000","numFound":1,"query":"name=Lieberman&limit=10&title=Exercised","limit":10,"start":0},"items":{"mods":{"@version":"3.8","titleInfo":{"title":"Exercised ","subTitle":"why something we never evolved to do is healthy and rewarding "},"name":{"@usage":"primary","@type":"personal","namePart":["Lieberman, Daniel E.,",{"@type":"date","#text":"1964-"}],"role":[{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"creator"}},{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"author"}}]},"typeOfResource":"text","genre":[{"@authority":"marcgt","#text":"bibliography"},{"@authority":"rdacontent","#text":"text"},{"@authority":"fast","#text":"Informational works"},{"@authority":"fast","#text":"History"},{"@authority":"lcgft","#text":"Informational works."},{"@authority":"rvmgf","#text":"Documents d'information."}],"originInfo":[{"place":{"placeTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"nyu"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"New York (State)"}]},"dateIssued":{"@encoding":"marc","#text":"2020"},"copyrightDate":{"@encoding":"marc","#text":"2020"},"issuance":"monographic","edition":"First edition."},{"@eventType":"publication","place":{"placeTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"New York :"}},"agent":{"namePart":"Pantheon Books,","role":{"roleTerm":"publisher"}},"dateIssued":[2020]}],"language":{"languageTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"eng"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"English"}]},"physicalDescription":[{"form":[{"@authority":"marcform","#text":"print"},{"@type":"media","@authority":"rdamedia","#text":"unmediated"},{"@type":"carrier","@authority":"rdacarrier","#text":"volume"}],"extent":"xix, 440 pages : illustrations, charts ; 25 cm"},{"form":{"@type":"Illustrative content","#text":"illustration"}}],"abstract":{"@type":"Summary","#text":"\"This highly engaging landmark work, a natural history of exercise--by the author of the best seller The Story of the Human Body--seeks to answer a fundamental question: were you born to run or rest? The first three parts of Exercised roughly follow the evolutionary story of human physical activity and inactivity, even as each chapter shatters a particular myth about exercise. Because we cannot understand physical activity without understanding its absence, Part One begins with physical inactivity. What are our bodies doing when we take it easy, including when we sit or sleep? Part Two explores physical activities that require speed, strength, and power, such as sprinting, lifting, and fighting. Part Three surveys physical activities that involve endurance, such as walking, running, or dancing, as well as their effect on aging. Part Four considers how anthropological and evolutionary approaches can help us exercise better in the modern world. How can we more effectively manage to exercise, and in what ways? To what extent, how, and why do different types and durations of exercise help prevent or treat the major diseases that are likely to make us sick and kill us?\"-- Provided by publisher."},"tableOfContents":{"@type":"Contents","#text":"Are we born to rest or run? -- Inactivity. Inactivity: the importance of being lazy ; Sitting: is it the new smoking? ; Sleep: why stress thwarts rest -- Speed, Strength, and Power. Speed: neither tortoise nor hare ; Strength: from brawny to scrawny ; Fighting and sports: from fangs to football -- Endurance. Walking: all in a day's walk ; Running and dancing: jumping from one leg to the other ; Endurance and aging: the active grandparent and costly repair hypotheses -- Exercise in the Modern World. To move or not to move: how to make exercise happen ; How much and what type? ; Exercise and disease -- Epilogue."},"note":[{"@type":"statement of responsibility","#text":"Daniel E. Lieberman."},{"@type":"bibliography","#text":"Includes bibliographical references (pages [343]-417) and index."}],"subject":[{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":["Exercise","History"]},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":["Physical fitness","History"]},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":["Physical education and training","History"]},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Exercise"},{"@authority":"mesh","topic":"Exercise"},{"@authority":"mesh","topic":["Physical Fitness","history"]},{"@authority":"mesh","topic":["Physical Eduation and Training","history"]},{"@authority":"rvm","topic":["Exercice","Histoire"]},{"@authority":"rvm","topic":["Éducation physique","Histoire"]},{"@authority":"rvm","topic":"Exercice"},{"@authority":"bisacsh","topic":"SCIENCE / Life Sciences / Evolution"},{"@authority":"bisacsh","topic":"SOCIAL SCIENCE / Anthropology / Physical"},{"@authority":"bisacsh","topic":"HEALTH & FITNESS / Exercise / General"},{"@authority":"fast","topic":"Exercise"},{"@authority":"fast","topic":"Physical education and training"},{"@authority":"fast","topic":"Physical fitness"},{"@authority":"sears","topic":"Exercise"},{"@authority":"sears","topic":"Physical fitness"},{"@authority":"sears","topic":"Physical education"}],"classification":[{"@authority":"lcc","#text":"QP301 .L628 2020"},{"@authority":"nlm","#text":"QT 256"},{"@edition":"23","@authority":"ddc","#text":"612.044"}],"relatedItem":[{"@type":"isReferencedBy","titleInfo":{"title":"Library Journal, August 2020"}},{"@type":"isReferencedBy","titleInfo":{"title":"Booklist starred, July 2020"}},{"@type":"isReferencedBy","titleInfo":{"title":"Publishers Weekly, September 2020"}},{"@type":"isReferencedBy","titleInfo":{"title":"Kirkus Review, July 2020"}},{"@type":"otherFormat","@otherType":"Online version:","@displayLabel":"Online version:","titleInfo":{"title":"Exercised"},"originInfo":{"publisher":"New York : Pantheon Books, [2020]"},"identifier":[{"@type":"isbn","#text":"9781524746995"},{"@type":"local","#text":"(DLC)  2020009067"}],"name":{"namePart":"Lieberman, Daniel E.,"}},{"@otherType":"HOLLIS record","location":{"url":"https://id.lib.harvard.edu/alma/99154306361003941/catalog"}}],"identifier":[{"@type":"isbn","#text":"9781524746988"},{"@type":"isbn","#t